1
00:00:02,200 --> 00:00:06,654
A real experiment. A language model is a program that continues text.

2
00:00:06,754 --> 00:00:10,482
We give one, GPT-2 XL, the start of a sentence, called the

3
00:00:10,582 --> 00:00:14,772
prompt: "I hate you because". Left alone, it continues like this.

4
00:00:14,866 --> 00:00:17,732
Now we run it again with the same random choices, but

5
00:00:17,832 --> 00:00:20,809
we add one fixed list of numbers inside the model while

6
00:00:20,909 --> 00:00:24,390
it reads. Same prompt, same model. Here is the new continuation.

7
00:00:24,466 --> 00:00:27,714
A list of numbers is called a vector. A vector that changes a model's

8
00:00:27,814 --> 00:00:31,110
behavior when you add it inside the model is called a steering vector.

9
00:00:31,200 --> 00:00:34,448
These two lines are the whole idea: the first builds a steering

10
00:00:34,548 --> 00:00:37,584
vector, the second adds it. By the end of this video, every

11
00:00:37,684 --> 00:00:41,252
symbol here will be defined from scratch, each with a worked example.

12
00:00:43,833 --> 00:00:48,736
A scalar is a single real number, like 3, minus one half, or 0.665.

13
00:00:48,836 --> 00:00:54,113
The set of all real numbers is written as a double-struck R. The rounded

14
00:00:54,213 --> 00:00:57,773
E reads "is an element of": 3 is an element of R.

15
00:00:58,866 --> 00:01:02,533
A vector is an ordered list of real numbers. Each entry is

16
00:01:02,633 --> 00:01:06,755
a component. The vector two, one has first component 2 and second

17
00:01:06,855 --> 00:01:10,718
component 1. Drawn as an arrow: two steps right, one step up.

18
00:01:10,799 --> 00:01:14,604
The number of components is the dimension, written d. All vectors

19
00:01:14,704 --> 00:01:17,428
with d real components form the set R to the d.

20
00:01:17,528 --> 00:01:19,531
So two, one is an element of R two.

21
00:01:20,599 --> 00:01:24,926
Inside GPT-2 XL, text is represented by vectors with d equal to

22
00:01:25,026 --> 00:01:30,969
1,600 components. They are computed using about 1.6 billion numbers, called parameters

23
00:01:31,069 --> 00:01:35,115
or weights, learned from text in a process called training.

24
00:01:38,500 --> 00:01:41,711
Take a equal to one, two, and b equal to three,

25
00:01:41,811 --> 00:01:46,150
minus one. Addition adds matching components: a plus b is four,

26
00:01:46,250 --> 00:01:49,392
one. In the picture, b's tail sits at a's tip.

27
00:01:49,466 --> 00:01:53,563
Multiplying by a scalar multiplies every component. Two times

28
00:01:53,663 --> 00:01:56,659
b is six, minus two: same direction, twice as

29
00:01:56,759 --> 00:01:59,686
long. A negative scalar flips the direction.

30
00:01:59,766 --> 00:02:03,500
Subtraction adds the flipped vector: a minus b is minus two,

31
00:02:03,600 --> 00:02:06,376
three, the arrow from the tip of b to the tip

32
00:02:06,476 --> 00:02:09,890
of a. The difference of two vectors is itself a vector.

33
00:02:10,966 --> 00:02:15,249
The Greek capital sigma means: add up every term. The mean, or

34
00:02:15,349 --> 00:02:19,208
average, of n vectors is their sum times one over n. The

35
00:02:19,308 --> 00:02:22,530
mean of one, two and three, four is two, three.

36
00:02:23,600 --> 00:02:29,128
The dot product multiplies matching components and adds the results into one scalar:

37
00:02:29,228 --> 00:02:33,014
a dot b is one times three plus two times minus one, which

38
00:02:33,114 --> 00:02:37,503
is 1. The norm, written with double bars, is a vector's length: the

39
00:02:37,603 --> 00:02:41,657
square root of the vector dotted with itself. The norm of b is

40
00:02:41,757 --> 00:02:46,012
the square root of ten, about 3.16. Dividing a vector by its norm

41
00:02:46,112 --> 00:02:49,564
gives a unit vector, of length one: a pure direction.

42
00:02:52,133 --> 00:02:56,220
A function takes each input to exactly one output. f from X to

43
00:02:56,320 --> 00:02:59,665
Y takes elements of a set X to elements of a set Y.

44
00:03:00,733 --> 00:03:06,264
A language model doesn't read letters. A tokenizer, a fixed function, cuts

45
00:03:06,364 --> 00:03:11,440
text into tokens, words or pieces of words, each with a whole-number

46
00:03:11,540 --> 00:03:15,702
ID. The set of all tokens is the vocabulary, V, and bars

47
00:03:15,802 --> 00:03:20,649
around a set mean its size. GPT-2's vocabulary has 50,257 tokens.

48
00:03:21,733 --> 00:03:25,723
Our prompt becomes these tokens. GPT-2 also starts every text

49
00:03:25,823 --> 00:03:29,478
with a special end-of-text token. A token's place in the

50
00:03:29,578 --> 00:03:32,697
sequence is its position, t, counting from zero.

51
00:03:33,766 --> 00:03:37,521
An embedding is a function from tokens to vectors: it looks up a

52
00:03:37,621 --> 00:03:41,195
learned vector in R d for each token and adds another for its

53
00:03:41,295 --> 00:03:45,894
position. In a toy, three-token vocabulary, "love" might map to one, zero. Now

54
00:03:45,994 --> 00:03:49,026
the text is a sequence of vectors, one per position.

55
00:03:51,600 --> 00:03:55,432
The model then processes these vectors in a fixed number

56
00:03:55,532 --> 00:03:58,732
of steps called layers. GPT-2 XL has L equal to

57
00:03:58,832 --> 00:04:01,892
48, numbered by ell from zero to L minus one.

58
00:04:02,966 --> 00:04:06,594
Each layer has two parts, both functions. Attention lets every

59
00:04:06,694 --> 00:04:10,983
position gather information from earlier positions; the MLP, a multilayer

60
00:04:11,083 --> 00:04:14,410
perceptron, transforms each position's vector on its own.

61
00:04:15,500 --> 00:04:19,147
Both add their outputs onto a running vector at every position:

62
00:04:19,247 --> 00:04:23,490
the residual stream. Leaving out a rescaling step called normalization, h

63
00:04:23,590 --> 00:04:26,762
sub ell plus one is h sub ell, plus attention's output,

64
00:04:26,862 --> 00:04:30,271
plus the MLP's output. As Elhage and colleagues put it, the

65
00:04:30,371 --> 00:04:33,484
residual stream is simply the sum of the output of all

66
00:04:33,584 --> 00:04:36,280
the previous layers and the original embedding.

67
00:04:36,966 --> 00:04:41,411
In two dimensions: the stream starts at one, zero. Attention writes

68
00:04:41,511 --> 00:04:45,345
zero, one, giving one, one. The MLP writes one half, minus

69
00:04:45,445 --> 00:04:48,194
one half, giving one and a half, one half.

70
00:04:48,866 --> 00:04:53,680
h sub ell, superscript t, is the residual stream entering layer ell

71
00:04:53,780 --> 00:04:59,034
at position t. Numbers computed inside a model like this are activations;

72
00:04:59,134 --> 00:05:03,288
one run through all layers is a forward pass. In GPT-2 XL,

73
00:05:03,388 --> 00:05:07,102
the stream entering layer 6 at "hate" has norm 88.7.

74
00:05:09,700 --> 00:05:12,942
To turn the final vector back into text, the model uses a

75
00:05:13,042 --> 00:05:17,164
matrix, a rectangular grid of numbers. Matrix times vector takes the dot

76
00:05:17,264 --> 00:05:20,448
product of each row with the vector: one number per row.

77
00:05:21,533 --> 00:05:24,187
The unembedding matrix, W sub U, has one row

78
00:05:24,287 --> 00:05:27,756
per vocabulary token. Times the final residual stream, it

79
00:05:27,856 --> 00:05:30,385
gives one score per token, called a logit.

80
00:05:31,466 --> 00:05:37,023
Softmax turns logits into probabilities: raise e to each logit,

81
00:05:37,123 --> 00:05:41,871
then divide by the total. Logits two, one, zero become

82
00:05:41,971 --> 00:05:46,630
0.665, 0.245 and 0.090: all positive, summing to one.

83
00:05:47,700 --> 00:05:51,424
The model then samples: it picks the next token at random

84
00:05:51,524 --> 00:05:56,390
with those probabilities, appends it, and repeats. After our prompt, GPT-2

85
00:05:56,490 --> 00:06:00,416
XL's most likely next token is "you", with probability 0.59.

86
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:05,600
Why would adding a vector change behavior? A unit

87
00:06:05,700 --> 00:06:09,457
vector describes a direction, and the linear representation hypothesis

88
00:06:09,557 --> 00:06:12,818
is the idea that high-level concepts are represented linearly

89
00:06:12,918 --> 00:06:15,188
as directions in some representation space.

90
00:06:16,266 --> 00:06:20,185
An early clue came from word vectors: Mikolov and colleagues reported

91
00:06:20,285 --> 00:06:23,389
that King minus Man plus Woman results in a vector very

92
00:06:23,489 --> 00:06:26,942
close to Queen. Woman minus Man acts like a gender direction.

93
00:06:27,633 --> 00:06:30,586
A toy picture: if one axis meant how positive the text

94
00:06:30,686 --> 00:06:33,865
is, moving a vector along it would make later layers treat

95
00:06:33,965 --> 00:06:37,541
the text as more positive. Steering looks for such directions and

96
00:06:37,641 --> 00:06:40,594
pushes along them. But it is a hypothesis: it can hold

97
00:06:40,694 --> 00:06:43,421
for some concepts and layers, and fail for others.

98
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:49,821
To find a direction, compare two inputs that differ mainly in

99
00:06:49,921 --> 00:06:53,486
one concept: a contrast pair, p plus and p minus. ActAdd,

100
00:06:53,586 --> 00:06:58,114
short for Activation Addition, by Turner and colleagues, uses "Love" and

101
00:06:58,214 --> 00:07:01,908
"Hate" and needs no training: a single pair of data points.

102
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:07,824
Run each prompt through the model, read the residual stream entering layer

103
00:07:07,924 --> 00:07:11,284
ell, and subtract: h A equals h ell of p plus, minus

104
00:07:11,384 --> 00:07:15,676
h ell of p minus, one difference per position. GPT-2 splits "Hate"

105
00:07:15,776 --> 00:07:19,004
into two tokens, so "Love" is padded with a space.

106
00:07:19,700 --> 00:07:23,953
One pair is noisy. CAA, Contrastive Activation Addition, by Rimsky and

107
00:07:24,053 --> 00:07:27,312
colleagues, uses a dataset D of triples: a prompt p, a

108
00:07:27,412 --> 00:07:31,230
completion c plus showing the behavior, and c minus showing its

109
00:07:31,330 --> 00:07:34,091
opposite. a sub ell of p and c is the residual

110
00:07:34,191 --> 00:07:37,264
stream at layer ell at the completion's last token.

111
00:07:37,933 --> 00:07:42,008
So: take each triple's difference of activations, add them up with

112
00:07:42,108 --> 00:07:45,044
sigma, and multiply by one over the size of D. A

113
00:07:45,144 --> 00:07:48,777
mean difference. ActAdd is the case where D holds one pair.

114
00:07:54,133 --> 00:07:57,755
Toy example: differences two point one, zero point three;

115
00:07:57,855 --> 00:08:00,954
one point eight, minus zero point five; two point

116
00:08:01,054 --> 00:08:04,023
one, zero point two. Their mean is exactly two,

117
00:08:04,123 --> 00:08:08,529
zero: the shared direction survives, the unrelated component cancels.

118
00:08:09,200 --> 00:08:12,429
In GPT-2 XL at layer 6, the "Love" and "Hate"

119
00:08:12,529 --> 00:08:16,349
activations have norms of about 98 and 97, and cosine

120
00:08:16,449 --> 00:08:20,714
similarity, the dot product of their unit vectors, of 0.72.

121
00:08:20,814 --> 00:08:25,079
Their difference has norm 73.8. At position zero both share

122
00:08:25,179 --> 00:08:29,740
the end-of-text token, so the difference there is exactly zero.

123
00:08:32,333 --> 00:08:36,562
Steering is one addition. During the forward pass, at layer ell and

124
00:08:36,662 --> 00:08:40,374
each chosen position t, h becomes h plus alpha times v, the

125
00:08:40,474 --> 00:08:45,285
steered activation h tilde. Alpha is a scalar, the steering coefficient: its

126
00:08:45,385 --> 00:08:49,937
size sets strength, its sign direction. The weights never change; in the

127
00:08:50,037 --> 00:08:54,073
ActAdd paper's words, these vectors act like virtual bias terms.

128
00:08:55,166 --> 00:08:58,339
T is the set of edited positions. ActAdd adds h

129
00:08:58,439 --> 00:09:01,682
A at the first positions of the prompt. CAA adds

130
00:09:01,782 --> 00:09:05,722
its vector at all token positions after the user's prompt.

131
00:09:06,399 --> 00:09:10,666
Toy example: h is one, one; v is two, zero; alpha is one

132
00:09:10,766 --> 00:09:15,579
and a half; so h tilde is four, one. With unembedding rows one,

133
00:09:15,679 --> 00:09:20,959
zero for "love", minus one, zero for "hate", and zero, one for "the",

134
00:09:21,059 --> 00:09:25,950
the logits go from one, minus one, one to four, minus four, one,

135
00:09:26,050 --> 00:09:29,771
and after softmax "love" rises from 0.47 to 0.95.

136
00:09:30,466 --> 00:09:33,764
Now GPT-2 XL with the original settings: "Love" minus

137
00:09:33,864 --> 00:09:38,316
"Hate", before layer 6, coefficient plus five. Next-token probabilities

138
00:09:38,416 --> 00:09:42,355
shift; "they" gains most. Then the first sampled continuations,

139
00:09:42,455 --> 00:09:46,458
same random choices, not cherry-picked; the original blog post's

140
00:09:46,558 --> 00:09:48,510
examples were the best of three.

141
00:09:51,100 --> 00:09:54,765
One example proves little, so we tested it, writing down our

142
00:09:54,865 --> 00:09:59,032
hypotheses and analysis before collecting any data. A first run used

143
00:09:59,132 --> 00:10:02,797
30 openings of real movie reviews; a second run, sized using

144
00:10:02,897 --> 00:10:06,687
