145
00:10:06,787 --> 00:10:09,072
six values of alpha, three times each.

146
00:10:09,766 --> 00:10:12,913
A control is a comparison condition identical except for

147
00:10:13,013 --> 00:10:16,161
the thing being tested. Ours adds random directions with

148
00:10:16,261 --> 00:10:18,306
the same norm as the steering vector.

149
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:22,901
A sentiment classifier, a separate model trained to judge how positive

150
00:10:23,001 --> 00:10:25,932
a text is, scored each continuation from zero to one.

151
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:31,647
In the larger run, average positivity was 0.66 without steering and 0.81

152
00:10:31,747 --> 00:10:35,999
at alpha five, while random directions of the same size gave 0.68.

153
00:10:36,099 --> 00:10:41,208
Error bars are 95 percent confidence intervals from the bootstrap: resample the

154
00:10:41,308 --> 00:10:45,429
prompts ten thousand times and keep the middle 95 percent of the

155
00:10:45,529 --> 00:10:49,913
averages. Steering minus no steering at alpha five is +0.15, with an

156
00:10:50,013 --> 00:10:54,924
interval from +0.10 to +0.19: our main hypothesis is supported, and steering

157
00:10:55,024 --> 00:10:59,342
beat random directions in both runs. But more is not always better:

158
00:10:59,442 --> 00:11:03,167
positivity peaked at alpha 10 and fell back at 15. And the

159
00:11:03,267 --> 00:11:07,255
negative alpha did not make text less positive. A check we did

160
00:11:07,355 --> 00:11:11,145
not plan in advance hints at why: at minus five, 29 percent

161
00:11:11,245 --> 00:11:16,223
